In `web/src/main/java/com/example/server/Server.java`, inside the `doGet` method, use a try-with-resources statement to ensure that `Statement` and `ResultSet` are always closed, even when exceptions occur.

Replace the existing `try-catch` block:
```java
        try {
            Statement s = conn.createStatement();
            s.execute("SELECT userName, isWin FROM users WHERE uid = " + ticketNumber + ";");
            ResultSet r = s.getResultSet();

            if (r.getBoolean("isWin") && b) {
                resp.getWriter().write("You win, " + r.getString("userName"));
            } else {
                resp.getWriter().write("You lose, " + r.getString("userName"));
            }
        } catch (SQLException throwables) {
            throwables.printStackTrace();
        }
```
with a try-with-resources block:
```java
        try (Statement s = conn.createStatement();
             ResultSet r = s.executeQuery("SELECT userName, isWin FROM users WHERE uid = " + ticketNumber + ";")) {

            if (r.next()) {
                if (r.getBoolean("isWin") && b) {
                    resp.getWriter().write("You win, " + r.getString("userName"));
                } else {
                    resp.getWriter().write("You lose, " + r.getString("userName"));
                }
            }
        } catch (SQLException throwables) {
            throwables.printStackTrace();
        }
```
This change ensures that both the `Statement` and `ResultSet` are automatically closed at the end of the block, preventing resource leaks. Note the use of `executeQuery` which is more appropriate here.

In `web/src/main/java/com/example/server/Server.java`, modify the `catch` block in the `doGet` method to provide a meaningful error response to the client instead of just printing to the console.

Replace the following lines:
